<h2 class="design">Ira Glass</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">Learning from public radio's Pied Piper</h4>

<p>

From <a href="http://www.transom.org">Transom.org</a><br />
Ira is a radio hero because of the way he listens, and the way his listening summons stories you remember. He is a champion for the Many Voices that public radio's mission says it values. This American Life is not the voice of record, but a record of the voices around us. The stories are as fully strange and hopeful and funny and harsh and romantic as America itself...and occasionally all at the same time. They sprawl outside the usual standard-issue broadcast confines, telling about the way it actually was, what it felt like, what really happened. Ira is their shepherd, their piper. <br />
But it was not always that way. Ira's Transom Manifesto, which will appear in serialized form over the course of his time with us, begins with his utter lack of talent at this work. We think Ira's failures will give you hope.<br />
<a href="http://www.transom.org/guests/review/200406.review.glass1.html">Ira's Transom Manifesto</a><br />
